{"t":1788428902,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"3c559c42-2f09-46c0-9ff7-8fe451531256","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-03T08:03:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 030803\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n403 AM EDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\n\nNo changes. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Slight Risk of severe thunderstorms today and Friday.\n\n- 2) Hot and humid conditions will persist through Friday.\n\n- 3) Drier conditions expected for the Labor Day weekend ahead. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Slight Risk of severe thunderstorms today and\nFriday.\n\nMultiple thunderstorm clusters will move from Pennsylvania east\nsoutheast into northern.northeast Maryland today through \ntonight along a frontal zone well defined on surface \nobservations and recent NUCAPS data. Given hot and unstable air \nmass south of this frontal zone and moderately strong flow \naloft, thunderstorms will be capable of producing damaging wind \ngusts. However, the forecast for today isn't clear cut with \nsatellite imagery showing strong upper level ridging over much \nof the area as per warm/dry water vapor signature on all three \nGOES WV channels.\n\nThe signal for convection is much stronger for Friday than it is\nfor today with large scale height falls forecast, stronger low-\nlevel convergence, and strengthening/stronger mid-level flow. \nBelieve the severe wx risk is greater tomorrow than it is today.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid conditions will persist through\nFriday.\n\nDeterministing and ensemble trends indicate MaxT today will be \na couple of degrees hotter than yesterday, but show a spread of \n2-3 degrees. Mid 90s seem likely and stayed away from any \nguidance showing upper 90s. Without any appreciable change in \nair mass, dewpoints should be in the low to mid 70s (72-73 \ndegrees), which is what has been observed the past couple of \ndays and stayed away from any guidance that was showing 75-77 \ndewpoints. Plugging 94 over 73 dewpoints yields max heat indices\nof 103.6F degrees. This is just shy of the heat advisory \ncriteria of 105, so heat indices of 100-105 is the most likely \nrange that we are going to experience today. Given the \nuncertainty in the MaxT range, will not make any changes to the\nHeat Advisory in effect. It will still be hot and humid \nnonetheless. Similar heat indices are expected Friday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nAs the cold front from earlier in the week tracks south of the \nregion, surface high pressure begins to move across the Great Lakes \nSaturday. Mid-level dry air settles over the Mid-Atlantic between \nSaturday and Sunday, allowing for heat & humidity to drop through \nthe weekend. Highs for Sunday range in the low 80s across much of \nthe area, with dewpoints ranging in the 50s and low-60s. Overall, it \nshould feel relatively mild & dry over the Labor Day holiday. \n\nThunderstorm chances drop during this period as well with \nincreasingly stable conditions. Looking towards early next week, \nupper-level ridging will begin to build over the Appalachians, \nallowing for relatively dry conditions to continue as temperatures \nstart to increase again by Tuesday. Thunderstorm chances remain low \nas this ridging builds overhead into next week.  \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /07Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\n\nMVFR/IFR cigs are expected this morning, then lifting quickly\nafter 14Z. \n\nNumerous thunderstorms expected today and Fri, some of which,\ncould be severe with damaging winds. Exact timing remains\nuncertain and will continue to handle TAFs with long PROB30\ngroups until things become clearer. \n\nPrevailing VFR conditions are anticipated at terminals through \nSunday & Monday. Northeast winds shift southeasterly by Monday \nevening. \n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\n\nSMWs may be needed again today especially north of the US-50\nbridge and everywhere Friday.\n\nWind gusts stay below 15 knots between Sunday and Monday. Northeast \nwinds shift southeasterly by Monday evening. \n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...\n\nA record daily high minimum of 73 degrees occurred at IAD \nyesterday Wed Sep 02. This broke the old record of 72 degrees\nset in 2020.\n\nRecord daily high maximums are not expected today or Friday as\nrecords are in the upper 90s to low 100s. However, record daily\nhigh minimums are still possible, but not likely, since\nwidespread convection is expected that should bring \ntemperatures close to their dewpoints. \n\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Heat Advisory from 11 AM this morning to 7 PM EDT this evening \n     for MDZ016>018.\nVA...Heat Advisory from 11 AM this morning to 7 PM EDT this evening \n     for VAZ029>031-036>040-050-051-055>057-501-502-526-527.\nWV...Heat Advisory from 11 AM this morning to 7 PM EDT this evening \n     for WVZ053.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...LFR/SRT\nAVIATION...LFR/SRT\nMARINE...LFR/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1788428869,"days":{"2026-09-03":{"hi":95,"lo":76,"pop":70,"short":"Isolated Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-04":{"hi":94,"lo":73,"pop":70,"short":"Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-05":{"hi":85,"lo":66,"pop":21,"short":"Mostly Sunny then Slight Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-06":{"hi":82,"lo":64,"pop":9,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-07":{"hi":83,"lo":65,"pop":2,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-08":{"hi":87,"lo":68,"pop":4,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-09":{"hi":90,"lo":null,"pop":9,"short":"Mostly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1788426000,"n":48,"ceil":[11000,11000,25000,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,10000,10000,10000,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[5.9,5.6,5.6,6.2,9.0,9.4,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,9.6,9.6,9.6,9